    <exercise xml:id="ex-hire-purchase-or-sacco-loan">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-hire-purchase-or-sacco-loan" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/14-commercial-arithmetic-iii/05-hire-purchase/Hire-purchase-or-SACCO-loan.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-hire-purchase">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-television">
            <statement><p>A television costs Ksh <m>27\,999</m> cash, or Ksh <m>4500</m> deposit and <m>10</m> monthly instalments of Ksh <m>2650</m>. Find the hire purchase price and the extra paid.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Hire purchase price Ksh <m>31\,000</m>; extra Ksh <m>3001</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-water-tank">
            <statement><p>A water tank costs Ksh <m>32\,000</m> cash. On hire purchase the deposit is <m>15\%</m> of the cash price, followed by <m>9</m> instalments of Ksh <m>3600</m>. Find the deposit, the hire purchase price and the extra paid.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Deposit Ksh <m>4800</m>; hire purchase price Ksh <m>37\,200</m>; extra Ksh <m>5200</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-cooker-instalment">
            <statement><p>A cooker's hire purchase price is Ksh <m>41\,400</m>: a deposit of Ksh <m>6000</m> and <m>12</m> equal monthly instalments. Find each instalment.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Ksh <m>2950</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-two-offers-solar">
            <statement><p>A solar kit costs Ksh <m>28\,500</m> cash. Offer A: Ksh <m>5000</m> deposit and <m>6</m> monthly instalments of Ksh <m>4200</m>. Offer B: Ksh <m>2000</m> deposit and <m>12</m> monthly instalments of Ksh <m>2400</m>. Which offer costs less, and how much more than the cash price does each cost?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>A costs Ksh <m>30\,200</m> and B Ksh <m>30\,800</m>, so A costs Ksh <m>600</m> less; extras Ksh <m>1700</m> and Ksh <m>2300</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>A: <m>5000 + 6 \times 4200 = 30\,200</m>, extra <m>30\,200 - 28\,500 = 1700</m>. B: <m>2000 + 12 \times 2400 = 30\,800</m>, extra <m>2300</m>. A is cheaper by <m>600</m>, but B asks for a smaller deposit and smaller monthly payments.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-deposit-needed">
            <statement><p>A bicycle shop's hire purchase price for a bicycle is Ksh <m>52\,500</m>, with <m>18</m> monthly instalments of Ksh <m>2600</m>. What deposit is paid?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>Ksh <m>5700</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The instalments total <m>18 \times 2600 = 46\,800</m>, so the deposit is <m>52\,500 - 46\,800 = 5700</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-hp-extra-percentage">
            <title>Challenge: Is the Extra Worth It?</title>
            <statement><p>A sewing machine costs Ksh <m>21\,000</m> cash, or Ksh <m>3000</m> deposit and <m>12</m> monthly instalments of Ksh <m>1800</m>. Find the extra as a percentage of the cash price, and give one good reason for choosing hire purchase anyway.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Find the hire purchase price first.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>Extra Ksh <m>3600</m>, <m>17.1\%</m> of the cash price. A tailor can earn money with the machine from the first month instead of saving for a year first.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>Hire purchase price <m>3000 + 12 \times 1800 = 24\,600</m>; extra <m>24\,600 - 21\,000 = 3600</m>; <m>\dfrac{3600}{21\,000} \times 100 = 17.1\%</m>. A good reason: the machine earns money from the start, which can pay the instalments.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-hire-purchase">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Activity parts (a) to (c) and <xref ref="example-motorbike-on-hire-purchase"/>. Give a card: <q>hire purchase price <m>=</m> deposit <m>+</m> instalments</q>. Assign <xref ref="ex-hp-television"/> and <xref ref="ex-hp-cooker-instalment"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-hp-television"/> to <xref ref="ex-hp-deposit-needed"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-hp-extra-percentage"/>. Extension: for the fridge in <xref ref="act-the-fridge-advert"/>, which is cheaper over a year, hire purchase or saving first and paying cash? What do you lose by waiting? (Discussion: saving first saves Ksh <m>5700</m> but means a year without the fridge.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: when is hire purchase a sensible choice, and when is it better to save first?</p>

